{
  "dom_id": "step-q88651",
  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "A card is drawn at random from a standard $52$-card deck. What is the probability of drawing a number card greater than $7$ or a non-spade card?\n\n*Hint: A standard $52$-card pack has four suits, each containing $13$ cards. Spades is one of these suits. Number cards are the cards labelled $2$ through $10$ in each suit. A jack, queen or king is not considered a number card.*",
  "worked_solution": "Let's call $G$ the event \"a card labeled with a number greater than $7$ is selected\" and $N$ the event \"a non-spade card is selected.\" \n\nThe required probability is $P(G\\cup N),$ which we can find using the addition law\n\n$$\nP(G\\cup N)=P(G)+P(N)-P(G\\cap N).\n$$\n\nLet's find $P(G)$ and $P(N).$\n\n- There are $12$ cards labeled with a number greater than $7$ in a standard deck. So, we have\n\n$$\nP(G)=\\frac{12}{52}=\\frac{3}{13}.\n$$\n\n- There are $39$ non-spade cards in a standard deck. So, we have\n\n$$\nP(N)=\\frac{39}{52}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\n- There are $9$ non-spade cards labeled with a number greater than $7.$ Indeed, the cards labeled with a number greater than $7$ are $8,$ $9,$ and $10,$ giving $3$ such cards per suit. Since there are $3$ non-spade suits, we have\n\n$$\nP(G\\cap N)=\\frac{9}{52}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of the known information into the addition law, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(G\\cup N) & =P(G)+P(N)-P(G\\cap N) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{13}+\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{9}{52} \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{26}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
